# How to cite Okto MultiWave Synth (GitHub shows it as "Cite this repository"). It is built on the Wavelet Synthesizer (its DOI is in references).
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: "Okto MultiWave Synth"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Guyot"
    given-names: "Pierre Arnaud"
    alias: "OktoPierre"
license: GPL-3.0-or-later
repository-code: "https://github.com/PierreArnaudGUYOT/Okto-MultiWave-Synth"
url: "https://pierrearnaudguyot.github.io/Okto-MultiWave-Synth/"
version: "1.0.4"
date-released: "2026-10-20"
references:
  - type: software
    title: "Wavelet Synthesizer"
    authors:
      - family-names: "Guyot"
        given-names: "Pierre Arnaud"
        alias: "OktoPierre"
    doi: 10.5281/zenodo.23431760
    repository-code: "https://github.com/PierreArnaudGUYOT/Free-Wavelet-Synthesizer-VST"
abstract: >-
  Okto MultiWave Synth is a polyphonic synthesizer with four wavelet oscillators (two subtractive, two additive) that shapes
  sound with the discrete wavelet transform instead of Fourier filters: one cycle of each tone is decomposed into 11
  octave-spaced wavelet bands and resynthesised band by band. Every oscillator has its own scale filter, position window,
  tuning, envelope and pitch envelope; a chord keyboard in the style of electronic chord harps plays whole chords with one finger.
  It is built on the Wavelet Synthesizer and ships over a thousand presets.
  Web application and VST3 / VST2 instrument for Windows x64.
keywords:
  - multi-oscillator synthesis
  - chord keyboard
  - sound synthesis
  - discrete wavelet transform
  - synthesizer
  - VST3
  - Web Audio
